---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../../styles/marketing.css';
import {comparisons} from '../../data/comparisons';

const title = 'Coding Agent Alternatives: Cloud vs Self-Hosted | GratefulAgents';
const description =
  'Compare cloud-managed coding agent harnesses with self-hosted options across the dimensions that actually matter: data residency, model choice, and control.';

const structuredData = [
  {
    '@type': 'CollectionPage',
    '@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/alternatives/#collection',
    name: title,
    description,
    url: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/alternatives/',
    author: {'@type': 'Organization', '@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/#organization'},
  },
  {
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    '@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/alternatives/#breadcrumb',
    itemListElement: [
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 1,
        name: 'Home',
        item: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 2,
        name: 'Alternatives',
        item: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/alternatives/',
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    '@type': 'ItemList',
    '@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/alternatives/#list',
    name: 'Coding agent alternatives compared',
    itemListElement: comparisons.map((c, i) => ({
      '@type': 'ListItem',
      position: i + 1,
      name: c.name,
      url: `https://gratefulagents.dev${c.href}`,
    })),
  },
];
---

<Base {title} {description} {structuredData}>
  <main id="main">
    <div class="mk-page">

      <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <ol class="mk-breadcrumb">
          <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
          <li><span aria-current="page">Alternatives</span></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <div class="mk-hero">
        <p class="section-kicker">Landscape Overview</p>
        <h1>Coding Agent Alternatives</h1>
        <p>
          The AI coding agent space has divided into two architectural camps: <strong>cloud-managed
          harnesses</strong> that run agent processes on the vendor's infrastructure, and
          <strong>self-hosted options</strong> that execute inside your own environment. The right
          choice depends on your organisation's requirements around data residency, model control,
          operational capacity, and trust model—not on any single product being objectively better.
        </p>
        <div class="cta-row">
          <a href="/self-hosted-coding-agents/" class="btn btn-primary">
            Why self-host? →
          </a>
          <a href="/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-kind/" class="btn btn-quiet">
            Try GratefulAgents
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mk-prose" style="max-width: none;">

        <h2>Two architectural camps</h2>
        <p>
          Cloud-managed coding agents—GitHub Copilot's coding agent and Devin being the most
          prominent examples—offer a compelling proposition: near-zero onboarding friction and a
          polished product experience managed entirely by the vendor. You connect your repositories,
          the vendor handles the infrastructure, and the agent gets to work. This model is genuinely
          superior for teams that want capable autonomous agents right now without provisioning
          servers or learning Kubernetes.
        </p>
        <p>
          The trade-off is that your source code, prompts, and model credentials all transit the
          vendor's network and execute on their infrastructure. For many teams this is acceptable.
          For teams with data-residency obligations, strict IP-protection policies, regulated
          environments, or private dependencies that cannot be reached from the public internet,
          it is not.
        </p>
        <p>
          Self-hosted options—including GratefulAgents and OpenHands—keep execution inside your
          own environment. The agent reads your code, calls your model APIs with your keys, and
          writes results back to your repositories, all without those intermediary steps crossing
          a third-party network boundary. The trade-off is operational responsibility: you own
          the cluster, the upgrades, and the incident response.
        </p>

        <h2>The axes that actually matter when choosing</h2>
        <p>
          Marketing comparisons tend to focus on feature checklists that go stale quickly and do
          not distinguish what is architecturally fundamental from what is a current product gap
          that could be closed in the next release. The dimensions below are more stable because
          they reflect underlying architecture, not surface feature sets.
        </p>

        <div class="mk-axis-list">
          <div class="mk-axis">
            <span class="mk-axis-label">Where execution happens</span>
            <span class="mk-axis-desc">
              Does the agent process run on your infrastructure or the vendor's? This determines
              whether your source code leaves your network during a run.
            </span>
          </div>
          <div class="mk-axis">
            <span class="mk-axis-label">Who holds credentials</span>
            <span class="mk-axis-desc">
              Model API keys and source control tokens are high-privilege credentials. Where are
              they stored, and who can access them?
            </span>
          </div>
          <div class="mk-axis">
            <span class="mk-axis-label">Model choice and cost</span>
            <span class="mk-axis-desc">
              Can you choose the model per-project, bring your own API keys, and apply your own
              volume contracts?
            </span>
          </div>
          <div class="mk-axis">
            <span class="mk-axis-label">Licence</span>
            <span class="mk-axis-desc">
              Is the harness open source? Can you read the code, audit what it does, and fork it
              if the vendor direction changes?
            </span>
          </div>
          <div class="mk-axis">
            <span class="mk-axis-label">Observability depth</span>
            <span class="mk-axis-desc">
              Can you trace every model call, tool invocation, token count, and cost, or are you
              limited to a high-level activity feed?
            </span>
          </div>
          <div class="mk-axis">
            <span class="mk-axis-label">Operational overhead</span>
            <span class="mk-axis-desc">
              How much infrastructure expertise is required to deploy, upgrade, and maintain the
              harness? Is there a managed option?
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2>Products compared</h2>
        <p>
          The following three comparisons examine the most common options teams consider when
          choosing a coding agent harness. Each page tries to be honest about where the competitor
          is the better choice—because the honest answer is that for some teams and some
          constraints, it is.
        </p>

        <div class="mk-alt-grid">
          {comparisons.map((c) => (
            <a href={c.href} class="mk-alt-card">
              <p class="mk-alt-tagline">{c.tagline}</p>
              <h3>GratefulAgents vs {c.name}</h3>
              <p>{c.summary}</p>
              <span class="mk-alt-link">Read comparison →</span>
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>

        <h2>Choosing honestly</h2>
        <p>
          There is no universally correct answer. Here is the clearest version of the decision:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>If your code already lives on GitHub.com and you have no data-residency
            constraints</strong>, GitHub Copilot's coding agent is the lower-friction choice.
            Zero infrastructure to manage, tight GitHub integration, capable and continuously
            improving.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>If you want a polished, fully managed autonomous agent and are comfortable
            with code leaving your network</strong>, Devin is worth evaluating. It is a
            thoughtfully designed product with serious engineering investment.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>If you want open source and self-hosted but do not want to take on
            Kubernetes</strong>, OpenHands is a strong option. Docker Compose, a large
            community, and a solid research track record.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>If you need a Kubernetes-native control plane with CRD-backed triggers,
            multi-platform clients, and a full Agent Ops observability console—all running
            inside your own cluster</strong>, that is what GratefulAgents is built for.
            It is in early development, so expect rough edges, but the architecture is designed
            for exactly this use case.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          For a deeper look at the architecture and requirements for self-hosting, see
          <a href="/self-hosted-coding-agents/">Self-Hosted Coding Agents: Architecture &amp; Evaluation Guide</a>.
          To try GratefulAgents, the <a href="/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-kind/">Kind
          quick-start guide</a> takes about fifteen minutes on a laptop.
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </main>
</Base>
